\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Convention}
\tag{ftip}
\title{Convex-hull grades and contribution evidence}

\p{Fix a recipient's declared initial endowment, a contribution transcript, a
research task and an independent evidence standard. A \newvocab{hull grade}
classifies the strongest contribution claim supported by that transcript. It
is a profile of the contribution's conceptual role, not a scalar ranking of the
contributor or a replacement for correctness, importance, difficulty,
attribution or cost.}

\p{Trajectory-level evidence for a claimed change of local idea basin follows
the [idea-basin and research-trajectory convention](ftip-00OV). The basin
descriptor and horizon qualify the evidence without changing the hull grades
below.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Inherited framework (0.5).} The transcript uses a method or framework
already admitted as part of the recipient's endowment. A contribution can still
be useful through explanation, execution or verification, but it adds no
supported hull level.}
\li{\strong{Rediscovery (1).} The recipient reconstructs a known method or result
without receiving its completed construction through the permitted channel.
The reconstruction must satisfy the frozen correctness contract.}
\li{\strong{Essential connection (2).} The recipient recognizes and applies a
relevant concept from another source, domain or representation. This is
\em{associative application}, and it is the basic form of \newvocab{conceptual
transfer}. The evidence must identify the source structure, the transfer map,
and a target result that depends on the connection.}
\li{\strong{New architecture (3).} The transcript supplies a composition or
organization of resources whose interface changes the method's operation. The
claim requires an essential dependency on that organization, together with
fresh-task evidence and a charged construction record.}
\li{\strong{Beyond.} The transcript supports a \newvocab{new conceptual
resource}: a precisely diagnosed obstruction, a portable reformulation, a
positive construction that fills the hole, consequences outside the original
target, and independent evidence that the resource was not supplied in the
starting endowment. This is the evidence standard for imaginative construction,
not a synonym for surprising wording or a high benchmark score.}
}

\p{The same transcript can receive two novelty labels. \em{Agent-relative
novelty} asks whether the resource was absent from the recipient's affordable
initial repertoire. \em{Historical novelty} asks whether the resource was new
relative to the declared mathematical or scientific background. Agent-relative
novelty can be substantial while historical novelty is zero; a historical
claim cannot be inferred from a learner's surprise. Report both labels and do
not use either as a proxy for truth.}

\p{A grade is assigned only after checking the relevant evidence. Retrieval,
transfer, remixing, construction, consequence and acquisition are separate
observations in the [research-capability profile](ftip-00OR). A transcript may
show a successful transfer and fail the construction standard, or show a new
construction whose proof is still unresolved. The final grade records the
highest supported level and the unresolved lower-level failures.}

\p{This convention refines the [convex-hull contribution proposal](https://gist.github.com/utensil/8850752d3d611cb8760b0fbaf724f256). It keeps the hull as an evidence-bearing classification while making the transfer and construction steps explicit. It does not assert that any current model or harness reaches a particular level.}
